          <p>Some updates on the home server. Recently, I set up a Minecraft
            server for me, my son, and his cousin to play together, an experience that was a big hit with the boys!</p>
          <p>Rediscovering Linux skills while setting up the Minecraft server was a refreshing experience. Though the
            initial setup was straightforward, it reminded me of the importance of regular practice. I stumbled over
            some basic commands, like "wget," and found myself relearning how to navigate between folders quickly in the
            terminal. Thankfully, I found helpful guidance from <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@KeepItTechie/videos"
              target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">KeepItTechie</a> on YouTube, whose tutorial was really easy to
            follow. I subscribed to the channel as part of my plan to keep practicing Linux.</p>
          <p>One of the challenges arose when my 5-year-old son struggled with using a keyboard and mouse to play
            Minecraft (he's more accustomed to using the Switch). Determined to find a solution, I explored how to add
            controller support, leading me to switch from the basic Minecraft Java server to a Forge server. That let my son run the Controllable mod without trouble. However, matching versions of Forge and mods between
            the server and client computers proved to be a bit tricky. I encountered various errors along the way, such
            as needing to kill Java because the old server was still running on the Minecraft port despite my attempts
            to stop and delete it.</p>
          <p>After troubleshooting and Googling solutions, everything fell into place. We had a blast yesterday when we
            created a large, pink horse farm on a cherry blossom village seed that I stumbled upon.</p>
          <p>Looking ahead, my oldest son occasionally plays Minecraft with his friends over Discord, sparking the idea
            of setting up a server for them as well. However, I anticipate challenges in ensuring simplicity for his
            friends to log on independently, as I won't always be available to assist with setup. I'm also not sure if
            my laptop is going to be sufficient to run additional servers on top of the music library, so I may need to
            look into other options as I move forward.</p>
          <p>Turning my old laptop into a home server continues to be filled with
            challenges and rewarding moments. From rediscovering Linux skills to overcoming Minecraft server challenges,
            each step reinforces the value of learning and exploration. Stay tuned for more updates as we explore new
            possibilities with our home server setup!</p>
          <p>Happy crafting!</p>
